Day 36 /100 of #DrGVishwanathanChallenge
Today's practice focused on taking Backtracking one step further by handling duplicate elements efficiently. 
Problem 1: LeetCode 90 – Subsets II
🔹 Approach
Sorted the input array so duplicate elements become adjacent.
Used Backtracking to generate all subsets.
Added the current subset to the answer at every recursive call.
While iterating, skipped duplicate elements using:
if(i > index && arr[i] == arr[i-1]) continue;
Backtracked after every recursive call by removing the last added element.
Time Complexity : O(N × 2ᴺ)
Space Complexity :  O(N) (Recursion stack, excluding output)
💡 Key Takeaways
1. Sorting simplifies duplicate handling.
2. Learned how to skip duplicate recursive branches.
3. Backtracking can efficiently generate unique subsets without using extra data structures.

Problem 2: LeetCode 40 – Combination Sum II
🔹 Approach
Sorted the candidates array.
Used Backtracking to build combinations.
Since each number can be used only once, recursive calls move to the next index (i + 1).
Skipped duplicate elements at the same recursion level.
Pruned the search whenever the current candidate became greater than the remaining target:
if(arr[i] > target) break;
because the array is sorted.
Time Complexity : O(2ᴺ) (Worst Case)
Space Complexity : O(N) (Recursion stack, excluding output)
💡 Key Takeaways
1. Sorting enables both duplicate removal and early pruning.
2. Learned the difference between Combination Sum I (reuse allowed) and Combination Sum II (each element used once).
3. Efficient pruning significantly reduces unnecessary recursive calls.
